WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.189 --> 00:00:14.455
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:14.896 --> 00:00:22.882
Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History, the talk show featuring exclusive interviews with famous people from

3
00:00:22.982 --> 00:00:23.842
the past.

4
00:00:24.443 --> 00:00:31.448
I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest was a writer, publisher, scientist, and inventor.

5
00:00:31.884 --> 00:00:37.593
But he might be best known for his role as one of the founding fathers of America.

6
00:00:38.093 --> 00:00:44.743
Please give a warm welcome to a man with an electrifying past—Benjamin Franklin!

7
00:01:01.016 --> 00:01:02.277
Why, thank you, Faye.

8
00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:05.218
It's a pleasure indeed to be here.

9
00:01:06.158 --> 00:01:08.839
It's so much fun to finally meet you, Ben.

10
00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.540
I see you've brought along a kite!

11
00:01:12.641 --> 00:01:19.684
I bet I know the story behind that actually, you accomplished so many things, you probably have lots

12
00:01:19.744 --> 00:01:20.344
of stories.

13
00:01:20.964 --> 00:01:22.525
I'm not sure where to begin!

14
00:01:23.245 --> 00:01:25.586
Why don't we start with your early years?

15
00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:28.267
What can you tell us about your childhood?

16
00:01:29.448 --> 00:01:38.395
Well, I was born in 1706 in Boston, Massachusetts, the fifteenth child in a family with 17 children.

17
00:01:39.836 --> 00:01:41.638
That's a big family!

18
00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:49.264
Indeed! I attended school for two years, but when I was about ten, my father had me work

19
00:01:49.344 --> 00:01:50.985
in his candle and soap shop.

20
00:01:51.446 --> 00:01:56.169
Unfortunately, I disliked the work—I wanted to be a sailor.

21
00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:01.782
Yet Father was worried I'd get lost at sea, so eventually he made me an apprentice in the

22
00:02:01.842 --> 00:02:04.243
printshop owned by my brother James.

23
00:02:04.684 --> 00:02:10.288
It wasn't my first choice for work, but Father knew I loved to read, and this allowed me

24
00:02:10.328 --> 00:02:12.549
to be around books all the time.

25
00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:20.955
You became a prolific writer later on, but I'm wondering with only two years at school, how did

26
00:02:20.995 --> 00:02:22.916
you become such a good writer?

27
00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.657
Did your father teach you?

28
00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:28.280
No, I practiced on my own.

29
00:02:28.992 --> 00:02:36.219
You see, Faye, I'd realized that writing well is a valuable skill, and I was determined to acquire

30
00:02:36.279 --> 00:02:44.026
it. So I taught myself how to write clearly by reading and memorizing many well-written articles, and then

31
00:02:44.086 --> 00:02:45.828
I wrote my own versions of them.

32
00:02:46.748 --> 00:02:52.754
I found the opportunity to start publishing my work when James began printing a newspaper.

33
00:02:53.535 --> 00:03:01.463
Without his knowledge, I submitted letters to the newspaper pretending to be a widow named Silence Dogood.

34
00:03:02.323 --> 00:03:06.185
Mrs. Dogood had strong opinions on just about everything.

35
00:03:07.065 --> 00:03:11.287
To my delight, the letters were very popular—Mrs.

36
00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:13.988
Dogood even received marriage proposals!

37
00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:18.730
Did your brother ever find out that you were Mrs.

38
00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:25.734
Dogood? He did, and it did not quite please him, but this was but one of many disputes

39
00:03:25.794 --> 00:03:26.314
we had.

40
00:03:26.875 --> 00:03:35.542
In 1723, after a particularly heated argument with James, I left Boston and wound up in Philadelphia, where

41
00:03:35.662 --> 00:03:36.963
I got a job as a printer.

42
00:03:37.664 --> 00:03:44.389
I continued writing, of course, publishing an almanac, Poor Richard's Almanack, for 25 years.

43
00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:52.135
At the time almanacs were very important to people because they included the yearly calendar and information about

44
00:03:52.155 --> 00:03:52.576
the weather.

45
00:03:53.072 --> 00:03:57.413
In mine I also published poems and witty articles.

46
00:03:58.213 --> 00:04:06.436
Oh, and I loved to dispense advice like, "Early to bed, and early to rise, makes a man

47
00:04:06.576 --> 00:04:09.097
healthy, wealthy, and wise."

48
00:04:11.477 --> 00:04:12.517
That's good advice!

49
00:04:13.098 --> 00:04:16.058
It sounds like you were very busy with literary activities.

50
00:04:16.499 --> 00:04:21.220
Yet you're also known for many significant contributions to science.

51
00:04:21.812 --> 00:04:25.035
When did you turn to science?

52
00:04:25.095 --> 00:04:31.080
By 1748 I had become wealthy enough to stop working at the printshop and explore other interests.

53
00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.802
And one of those interests was science.

54
00:04:34.543 --> 00:04:41.509
For example, I was curious about the strong Atlantic Ocean current that moves along the east coast, which—

55
00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:45.112
Oh! That's called the Gulf Stream, right?

56
00:04:46.232 --> 00:04:46.993
Why, yes.

57
00:04:48.094 --> 00:04:50.136
I was the first to map it for sailors.

58
00:04:50.704 --> 00:04:56.866
I was also interested in medicine, so I helped to establish the first hospital in Philadelphia, and wrote

59
00:04:56.906 --> 00:04:58.866
about ideas related to health.

60
00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:06.569
For instance, I realized that people caught colds from close contact with one another, not from damp air

61
00:05:06.689 --> 00:05:09.210
or clothing as others believed at the time.

62
00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:16.252
Yet perhaps my most important contribution to science was what I learned from flying a kite during a

63
00:05:16.392 --> 00:05:22.657
thunderstorm. I wanted to test the idea that lightning was a form of electricity.

64
00:05:23.098 --> 00:05:28.959
So in June 1752, I tied a metal key to the string of a kite with a metal

65
00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:31.600
rod on top, and headed out into a storm.

66
00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:37.822
Sure enough, I felt a spark—electricity traveled from the rod to the key!

67
00:05:39.142 --> 00:05:42.163
Whoa, Ben, that sounds really dangerous!

68
00:05:43.023 --> 00:05:50.017
Yes, it was a very foolish thing to do, but it helped me invent my lightning rod.

69
00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:59.540
I invented a great many things, you know, like bifocal glasses and the Franklin stove, which was an

70
00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.341
improvement upon a normal fireplace.

71
00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:08.643
I also invented— I don't think we can hear about all your inventions, Ben—there are too many!

72
00:06:08.663 --> 00:06:12.184
I'm sure your inventions made you even richer.

73
00:06:13.432 --> 00:06:19.835
Actually, Faye, I never got a patent for any of my inventions—I was glad to have the opportunity

74
00:06:19.875 --> 00:06:21.856
to freely give them to the public.

75
00:06:22.276 --> 00:06:28.718
I even helped to create many fine public institutions such as the first public fire department and lending

76
00:06:28.779 --> 00:06:33.961
library. That's so generous of you, Ben!

77
00:06:34.921 --> 00:06:39.703
And speaking of the public you had quite a public service career, didn't you?

78
00:06:40.323 --> 00:06:41.604
Yes, that's true.

79
00:06:42.388 --> 00:06:45.529
I was very active in Pennsylvania politics.

80
00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:49.831
I was even sent to England to speak on behalf of the American colonies.

81
00:06:50.492 --> 00:06:57.855
In 1776, I joined Thomas Jefferson and several others to draft the Declaration of Independence.

82
00:06:58.515 --> 00:07:05.778
This document stated that America was free from British rule and would now be called the United States.

83
00:07:06.378 --> 00:07:09.760
Later that year I went to France to serve as an ambassador.

84
00:07:10.912 --> 00:07:12.353
What about the Constitution?

85
00:07:12.814 --> 00:07:14.415
Were you involved in that as well?

86
00:07:15.516 --> 00:07:20.800
I signed that document too, much later though, in 1787.

87
00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:28.727
I was invited to attend the Constitutional Convention in Philadelphia where representatives from the states discussed laws that

88
00:07:28.767 --> 00:07:30.769
would bind the country together.

89
00:07:30.789 --> 00:07:38.355
Alas, by then I was 81, an old man who had to be carried into the meeting on

90
00:07:38.395 --> 00:07:38.896
a chair.

91
00:07:39.468 --> 00:07:45.010
But I was still able to contribute ideas to the founding of the new democracy.

92
00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:53.953
You made so many contributions, Ben, from science to publishing to politics and public institutions.

93
00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:57.854
Thank you for all of that—and for being on the show today.

94
00:07:58.994 --> 00:08:01.335
You've been a very kind host, Faye.

95
00:08:01.875 --> 00:08:10.563
Farewell! Benjamin Franklin died in 1790 at the age of 84.

96
00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:20.789
Through his writing, inventions, scientific experiments, and public service, Ben played an enormous role in shaping America's history,

97
00:08:20.849 --> 00:08:22.671
technology, and culture.

98
00:08:23.351 --> 00:08:25.953
I hope you enjoyed my interview with Benjamin Franklin.

99
00:08:26.653 --> 00:08:30.716
I'll be back soon with another fascinating guest from the past.

100
00:08:31.356 --> 00:08:35.359
You've been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History!
